    content: `<h2>Packet Intervals From 1.0 ms Down to 0.125 ms</h2>
<p>Polling rate measures how frequently your USB host controller asks your mouse sensor for newly accumulated movement counts and button clicks. At standard <code>1000Hz</code>, updates arrive every <code>1.000 ms</code>. Stepping up to <code>2000Hz</code> halves that gap to <code>0.500 ms</code>, <code>4000Hz</code> drops it to <code>0.250 ms</code>, and <code>8000Hz</code> tightens the reporting interval to <code>0.125 ms</code>.</p>
<p>On paper, shrinking the worst-case interval between sensor capture and frame render lowers average input latency by roughly <code>0.375 ms</code> (going from 1000Hz's 0.50 ms average wait to 4000Hz's 0.125 ms average wait). That sub-millisecond timing edge will never replace real human speed—benchmark your own baseline in our <a href="/reaction-time-test/">Reaction Time Test</a> for perspective—yet polling stability plays a real role in <strong>visual motion smoothness</strong>.</p>
<p>When a <code>1000Hz</code> mouse feeds a <code>360Hz</code> (<code>2.78 ms</code> frame) or <code>480Hz OLED</code> (<code>2.08 ms</code> frame) display, the asynchronous timing drift between 1 ms packets and 2–3 ms frames causes alternating 2-packet and 3-packet renders. That unevenness appears as faint micro-stutter when panning smoothly across thin vertical walls. At <code>4000Hz</code> (10–11 packets per frame), frame pacing smooths out dramatically.</p>

<h2>The DPI Saturation Problem on High Polling Rates</h2>
<p>Buying an 8K dongle while running <code>400 DPI</code> wastes almost every extra polling slot. A USB frame only carries meaningful motion if your hand moved far enough in the preceding <code>0.125 ms</code> to generate at least one sensor count.</p>
<p>Consider a slow long-range micro-tracking swipe moving at <code>5 cm/s</code> (<code>1.97 in/s</code>):</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>At 400 DPI:</strong> Your sensor generates <code>400 × 1.97 ≈ 787 counts/second</code>. Out of <code>8,000</code> polling intervals per second, over <strong>90% transmit zero motion</strong>, producing lumpy bursts instead of smooth updates.</li>
<li><strong>At 1600 DPI:</strong> Your sensor generates <code>1600 × 1.97 ≈ 3,150 counts/second</code>—quadrupling packet density during delicate tracking while keeping identical physical <code>cm/360°</code> when you divide in-game sensitivity by four.</li>
</ul>
<p>Scale your in-game sensitivity cleanly when bumping hardware DPI in our <a href="/dpi/">DPI &amp; eDPI Calculator</a>, then check real CPI accuracy in the <a href="/dpi-analyzer/">Mouse DPI Analyzer</a>.</p>

<div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Polling Rate</th><th>Report Interval</th><th>Avg Sync Latency</th><th>Min Recommended DPI</th><th>Typical Wireless Battery</th><th>Best Use Case</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1000Hz</td><td>1.000 ms</td><td>~0.50 ms</td><td>400–800 DPI</td><td>70–95 hrs</td><td>Older CPUs, 144Hz panels, travel endurance</td></tr><tr><td>2000Hz</td><td>0.500 ms</td><td>~0.25 ms</td><td>800–1600 DPI</td><td>35–50 hrs</td><td>Optimal battery/smoothness sweet spot</td></tr><tr><td>4000Hz</td><td>0.250 ms</td><td>~0.125 ms</td><td>1600 DPI</td><td>20–28 hrs</td><td>240Hz–540Hz competitive FPS</td></tr><tr><td>8000Hz</td><td>0.125 ms</td><td>~0.063 ms</td><td>1600–3200 DPI</td><td>12–18 hrs</td><td>Wired/dongle showcase on high-end CPUs + OLED</td></tr></tbody></table></div>

<h2>CPU Interrupt Overhead and Frame-Time Stability</h2>
<p>High polling is not free for your processor. Processing 8,000 HID packets every second consumes meaningful single-core interrupt cycles—especially if your game lacks efficient raw input batching. In titles without <code>RawInputBuffer</code> enabled (or older DirectX 11 builds), swiping rapidly at <code>8000Hz</code> can spike frame times and cut <code>1% Low</code> FPS by 15%–30% on mid-range CPUs.</p>
<p>Always plug high-speed wireless dongles directly into rear motherboard USB 3.x ports routed straight to the CPU (avoiding unpowered monitor hubs or front-panel extensions) and enable <strong>Raw Input Buffer</strong> inside Valorant or CS2.</p>

<h2>Display Refresh Thresholds: 144Hz vs. 360Hz+ OLED</h2>
<p>On a <code>144Hz</code> or <code>165Hz</code> LCD monitor (<code>6.06–6.94 ms</code> frame time), <code>1000Hz</code> already packs six to seven mouse updates into every single display frame; jumping to <code>4000Hz</code> brings virtually zero visual gain. The smoothness benefit emerges once you cross <code>240Hz</code> and becomes clearest on ultra-fast <code>360Hz</code> to <code>540Hz</code> OLED/DyAc displays where individual pixel persistence is under <code>0.5 ms</code>.</p>

<h2>Practical Recommendation: Where 2000Hz–4000Hz Wins</h2>
<p>For the vast majority of competitive players on modern 8-core CPUs and 240Hz+ monitors, <strong><code>2000Hz</code> or <code>4000Hz</code> paired with <code>1600 DPI</code></strong> delivers 98% of the motion-clarity upside with zero game-engine hitching and double the battery life of 8000Hz. Save 8000Hz for wired setups or tournament matches where you plug in to charge between every map.</p>
<p>Also keep your 2.4GHz receiver dongle positioned cleanly on your desk using the bundled USB extension cable, sitting 15–25 cm from the top edge of your mousepad away from Wi-Fi routers and metal PC cases. Wireless packet drops caused by RF interference destroy the consistency advantage of 4000Hz far faster than dropping back to a rock-solid 1000Hz link.</p>`
