    content: `<h2>Same cm/360°, Different Input Pipeline Behavior</h2>
<p>A common misconception claims that <code>400 DPI × 2.0</code> and <code>1600 DPI × 0.5</code> in Counter-Strike 2 feel completely identical because both yield <code>800 eDPI</code> (<code>51.95 cm/360°</code>). While your full-turn physical distance matches to the millimeter, the <strong>input granularity and start-of-motion latency</strong> entering your PC differ substantially.</p>
<p>Every game engine multiplies incoming integer mouse counts by your in-game sensitivity multiplier. Think of DPI as the sample resolution of a ruler and in-game sensitivity as the zoom multiplier applied afterward. Doubling DPI and halving sensitivity feeds four times as many fine-grained angle steps into the engine across the exact same physical sweep.</p>
<p>You can compute exact matched sensitivities across 400, 800, 1600, and 3200 DPI in seconds with our <a href="/dpi/">DPI &amp; eDPI Calculator</a>.</p>

<h2>First-Count Thresholds and Motion-Start Latency</h2>
<p>An optical mouse cannot inform Windows that your hand started moving until you cross at least <strong>one full count</strong> of sensor distance. Before that threshold passes, zero USB motion packets leave the mouse.</p>
<p>Look at how far your hand must physically travel from a dead stop before the sensor fires its very first count:</p>

<div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Hardware DPI</th><th>Distance Per 1 Count</th><th>CS2 Yaw Step @ 800 eDPI</th><th>Measured Start-of-Motion Latency (Slow Swipe)</th><th>Polling Saturation (2K–4K)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>400 DPI</td><td>0.0635 mm (63.5 µm)</td><td>0.044° per step</td><td>~3.5–4.5 ms baseline delay</td><td>Poor (many empty frames)</td></tr><tr><td>800 DPI</td><td>0.0318 mm (31.8 µm)</td><td>0.022° per step</td><td>~1.8–2.5 ms</td><td>Good for fast flicks, weak on micro</td></tr><tr><td>1600 DPI</td><td>0.0159 mm (15.9 µm)</td><td>0.011° per step</td><td>~0.8–1.2 ms (1–2 ms faster than 400)</td><td>Strong across all speeds</td></tr></tbody></table></div>

<p>Measured with high-speed oscilloscope rigs during slow-to-medium flick starts, switching from <code>400 DPI</code> to <code>1600 DPI</code> trims roughly <strong><code>1.5 ms to 2.5 ms</code></strong> off initial motion registration—equivalent to upgrading an entire generation of wireless latency for free.</p>

<h2>Pixel Skipping vs. Angular Quantization Reality</h2>
<p>Years of forum myths warned about "pixel skipping" at 400 DPI. Strictly speaking, modern 3D shooters do not snap to 2D monitor pixels; they rotate the camera sphere by discrete angular increments (<code>yaw × sensitivity</code>).</p>
<p>At <code>400 DPI</code> and <code>2.0</code> CS2 sensitivity, every single mouse count rotates your view by <code>0.044°</code>—roughly 1.5 to 2 screen pixels per count on a 1080p 16:9 display. During slow micro-tracking or scoped AWP/Operator holds across long corridors, moving in <code>0.044°</code> stairs looks faintly notched compared to <code>1600 DPI</code>'s buttery <code>0.011°</code> micro-steps.</p>

<h2>Why Legacy 400 DPI Habits Persist Among Tactical Pros</h2>
<p>If 1600 DPI wins mathematically on latency and smoothness, why do half the players in our <a href="/pros/">Pro Player Settings Database</a> still run <code>400</code> or <code>800 DPI</code>?</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Buy-menu and inventory speed:</strong> CS2's buy wheel and loot menus use your raw Windows desktop DPI. Veterans with ten years of 400 DPI cursor memory dislike relearning menu clicks at 4× speed.</li>
<li><strong>Unintentional micro-movement masking:</strong> Because <code>400 DPI</code> ignores hand twitches smaller than <code>0.063 mm</code> while resting a finger on Mouse1, some players feel slightly "steadier" holding pixel angles—essentially using coarse sensor quantization as a deadzone filter.</li>
<li><strong>Tournament habit inertia:</strong> Changing anything on LAN invites doubt, so players stick with what carried them to Tier 1.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Migrating Safely From 400 or 800 to 1600 DPI</h2>
<p>Switching to <code>1600 DPI</code> takes five minutes to feel natural and fixes both first-count latency and 2000Hz–4000Hz polling saturation. To preserve your Windows and buy-menu cursor speed during the jump from <code>800 DPI</code> to <code>1600 DPI</code>, set your Windows Pointer Speed slider down two notches from <code>6/11</code> (1.0×) to <code>4/11</code> (0.5×)—every Raw Input FPS title ignores the Windows slider completely, while your desktop and menus feel identical.</p>
<p>Should you push even higher to 3200 or 6400 DPI? On modern PixArt PAW3950 and HERO 2 sensors, there is no harmful smoothing at 3200 DPI, yet diminishing returns hit hard: first-count distance shrinks by only another 8 micrometers while buy-menu cursors become frantic unless heavily scaled down. Always verify your new 1600 DPI step in the <a href="/dpi-analyzer/">Mouse DPI Analyzer</a> afterward, as some sensors show slightly different CPI deviation at 1600 DPI than at 400 DPI.</p>`
