    content: `<h2>Beyond Peak DPI Marketing: What Actually Separates Flagship Sensors</h2>
<p>Peak numbers like <code>30,000 DPI</code>, <code>35,000 DPI</code>, or <code>44,000 DPI</code> have zero competitive value—nearly every elite player in our <a href="/pros/">Pro Player Settings Database</a> runs between <code>400</code> and <code>3200 DPI</code>. Once optical sensors cleared the <code>650 IPS</code> threshold years ago, raw malfunction speed stopped deciding gunfights.</p>
<p>What genuinely separates the three dominant flagship optical engines—<strong>PixArt PAW3950</strong>, <strong>Razer Focus Pro 35K Gen-2</strong>, and <strong>Logitech HERO 2</strong>—is firmware polish: how cleanly they handle frosted glass mousepads, how finely you can tune Lift-Off Distance (LOD), how little power they draw at 4000Hz polling, and how tightly MCU firmware syncs SPI reads to USB frames.</p>

<div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Specification</th><th>PixArt PAW3950</th><th>Razer Focus Pro 35K Gen-2</th><th>Logitech HERO 2</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Max DPI</td><td>30,000 DPI</td><td>35,000 DPI (1-DPI steps)</td><td>44,000 DPI (firmware updated)</td></tr><tr><td>Max Tracking Speed / Accel</td><td>750 IPS / 50G</td><td>750 IPS / 70G</td><td>888 IPS / 88G</td></tr><tr><td>Glass Tracking Support</td><td>Native (≥4 mm thickness)</td><td>Smart Tracking on clear/frosted glass</td><td>Good on textured/coated glass; selective on clear</td></tr><tr><td>Lift-Off Distance Tuning</td><td>0.7 mm / 1.0 mm / 2.0 mm</td><td>26-step Asymmetric Cut-Off</td><td>Low / Medium / High (3 presets)</td></tr><tr><td>Polling &amp; Sync Feature</td><td>Up to 8000Hz + MotionSync</td><td>8000Hz HyperPolling + Sensitivity Matcher</td><td>8000Hz Lightspeed + Zero Smoothing</td></tr></tbody></table></div>

<h2>PixArt PAW3950: Open Flagship Tracking and Glass Readiness</h2>
<p>Found inside lightweight favorites from Pulsar, Lamzu, WLmouse, Endgame Gear, and VAXEE, the <strong>PAW3950</strong> improved upon the older PAW3395 in two practical areas: native glass tracking and tighter power efficiency. PixArt lowered the minimum Lift-Off Distance floor down to <code>0.7 mm</code>, giving low-sensitivity arm aimers crisp mouse lifts with minimal cursor drop.</p>
<p>Because dozens of brands implement the PAW3950 with different MCUs (Nordic nRF52840 vs. newer high-clock wireless controllers), sensor feel still depends heavily on each brand's firmware. Look for implementations offering clean <code>MotionSync</code> toggles and native web-based CPI tuning.</p>

<h2>Razer Focus Pro 35K Gen-2: Asymmetric LOD and Sensitivity Matching</h2>
<p>Developed exclusively alongside PixArt inside flagship shells like the Viper V3 Pro and DeathAdder V3 HyperSpeed, the <strong>Focus Pro 35K Gen-2</strong> adds fine-grained software control you will not find on standard PAW3950 mice:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Asymmetric Cut-Off (26 levels):</strong> Set separate lift-off and landing distances—for example, cutting tracking at <code>1.0 mm</code> lift while waiting until <code>0.6 mm</code> landing to re-engage, eliminating downward cursor drift when slamming the mouse back onto the pad.</li>
<li><strong>1-DPI stepping &amp; Sensitivity Matcher:</strong> Compensate for hardware CPI deviation directly in Synapse down to single-DPI steps so your real physical distance matches your old mouse.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Logitech HERO 2: Dual-Array Efficiency and 888 IPS Headroom</h2>
<p>Built in-house inside the G Pro X Superlight 2 and Dex, <strong>HERO 2</strong> pairs a dual-optical sensor array with adaptive frame-rate scaling. Rather than burning full illumination power constantly, HERO 2 ramps capture frequency seamlessly as hand acceleration spikes—delivering <code>888 IPS</code> and <code>88G</code> tracking headroom while stretching wireless battery life noticeably longer than rival 4K/8K mice.</p>
<p>Its limitation sits in surface tuning: Logitech offers three broad LOD presets rather than Razer's 26 micro-steps, and ultra-clear untreated glass can still occasionally require a textured glass pad (see surface compatibility in our <a href="/mousepad-chart/">Mousepad Friction &amp; Speed Chart</a>).</p>

<h2>MotionSync, Lift-Off Distance, and Surface Calibration</h2>
<p>Two quick setup rules apply across all three sensors. First, keep <strong>MotionSync off at 1000Hz</strong> if you chase rock-bottom click-to-pixel latency (avoiding up to ~0.8 ms buffering), or enable it only at <code>2000Hz–4000Hz</code> where sync overhead drops below <code>0.25 ms</code>. Second, after picking your pad and skates, run a quick ruler check in the <a href="/dpi-analyzer/">Mouse DPI Analyzer</a>—even the best flagship silicon ships with ±3% lens variance that takes 60 seconds to calibrate away.</p>
<p>Finally, if you already own a well-implemented PAW3395 or HERO 25K mouse whose shape fits your hand like a glove, do not expect a miracle rank jump purely from swapping to a 35K or 3950 sensor. Upgrade for lighter shell weight, better coating, or native glass tracking rather than chasing raw IPS numbers you will never physically approach.</p>`
